1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,714
Coach: Begin a fresh excerpt.

2
00:00:01,714 --> 00:00:07,714
Coach: The group is still planning one twenty-minute welcome activity in the main room,

3
00:00:07,714 --> 00:00:10,714
Coach: using existing paper and pens without purchases.

4
00:00:10,714 --> 00:00:16,286
Coach: It should help newcomers meet and find out about the weekly drawing group.

5
00:00:16,636 --> 00:00:20,922
Maya: We could start in pairs and exchange names and interests.

6
00:00:25,272 --> 00:00:27,415
Coach: There is a short pause.

7
00:00:27,415 --> 00:00:29,558
Coach: Noah has not replied yet.

8
00:00:29,558 --> 00:00:33,415
Coach: That is all the excerpt establishes about his response.

9
00:00:33,415 --> 00:00:36,415
Coach: Four seconds was chosen for this illustration;

10
00:00:36,415 --> 00:00:41,558
Coach: it is not a rule for when a real discussion needs intervention.

11
00:00:41,908 --> 00:00:44,479
Coach: You could allow more thinking space.

12
00:00:44,479 --> 00:00:49,622
Coach: If you do speak, consider what the group still needs to explore.

13
00:00:49,622 --> 00:00:51,765
Coach: The connection between paired introductions

14
00:00:51,765 --> 00:00:56,479
Coach: and learning about the drawing group has not yet been explained.

15
00:00:56,829 --> 00:01:02,400
Coach: An open question about that connection gives others something concrete to respond to.

16
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:06,686
Coach: It need not demand that one named person speak immediately,

17
00:01:06,686 --> 00:01:11,829
Coach: and it should not announce a decision that no one has confirmed.

18
00:01:12,179 --> 00:01:16,465
Coach: Privately identify what you know and what you do not.

19
00:01:16,465 --> 00:01:19,893
Coach: You know the proposal and the two aims.

20
00:01:19,893 --> 00:01:26,322
Coach: You do not know why the pause occurred or whether the others accept the idea.

21
00:01:34,672 --> 00:01:37,672
Coach: Compare three next turns for this moment.

22
00:01:37,672 --> 00:01:41,529
Coach: Think about which gives the group a useful opening

23
00:01:41,529 --> 00:01:43,244
Coach: while leaving opinions unassumed.

24
00:01:43,244 --> 00:01:50,101
Coach: A response that works after clear agreement may not fit a pause before anyone has responded.

25
00:01:50,451 --> 00:01:53,880
Coach: The replies are possible continuations written for practice.

26
00:01:53,880 --> 00:01:57,737
Coach: Look at what each opening asks others to do.

27
00:01:57,737 --> 00:02:02,023
Coach: It does not tell us what every person would feel

28
00:02:02,023 --> 00:02:04,594
Coach: or how every group would respond.

29
00:02:04,944 --> 00:02:06,658
Coach: Keep the purpose small:

30
00:02:06,658 --> 00:02:12,230
Coach: offer a next point, rather than secure a final decision in one sentence.

31
00:02:12,230 --> 00:02:17,373
Coach: An invitation to discuss an idea is not agreement to adopt it.

32
00:02:25,723 --> 00:02:32,580
You: How could the paired introductions also help new members find out about the weekly drawing group?

33
00:02:32,930 --> 00:02:38,073
Noah: We could each draw or write one thing we'd like to try,

34
00:02:38,073 --> 00:02:40,644
Noah: then use that to introduce ourselves.

35
00:02:40,994 --> 00:02:46,137
Coach: The question makes an open part of the task available for discussion.

36
00:02:46,137 --> 00:02:49,137
Coach: Noah offers another idea in this example.

37
00:02:49,137 --> 00:02:55,566
Coach: Neither proposal yet tells us how accurate information about the drawing group will be provided,

38
00:02:55,566 --> 00:02:57,280
Coach: so that work remains.

39
00:02:57,630 --> 00:03:01,487
Coach: The pause alone does not choose the next move.

40
00:03:01,487 --> 00:03:06,202
Coach: Combine what you observe with what remains open in the task.

41
00:03:06,202 --> 00:03:07,916
Coach: You can leave space,

42
00:03:07,916 --> 00:03:13,059
Coach: or offer a question that lets the group examine a specific point.

43
00:03:13,409 --> 00:03:18,552
Coach: After speaking, make room for a reply and attend to its content.

44
00:03:18,552 --> 00:03:22,838
Coach: Another proposal may need comparison; a question may need clarification;

45
00:03:22,838 --> 00:03:25,838
Coach: continued silence may call for more space.

46
00:03:25,838 --> 00:03:28,409
Coach: The phrase itself cannot guarantee progress.

47
00:03:28,759 --> 00:03:34,759
Coach: Privately form one possible next turn for the original pause, or choose to wait.

48
00:03:34,759 --> 00:03:38,616
Coach: Keep the purpose clear and the others’ views open.

49
00:03:38,616 --> 00:03:41,616
Coach: Pause playback if you want more time.

50
00:03:49,966 --> 00:03:53,823
Coach: If you formed a reply, check what it assumes.

51
00:03:53,823 --> 00:03:59,823
Coach: Does it offer a task-related opening, or put words into someone else’s mouth?

52
00:03:59,823 --> 00:04:07,109
Coach: Next, a fresh excerpt will examine what to do when two turns begin before either is finished.
